---
// src/pages/events.astro - the events calendar.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE SHAPE OF THE PAGE, AND WHY
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Two columns on a wide screen: a real month-grid CALENDAR on the left, and an
// info PANEL on the right for whatever is selected in it. On a phone they stack -
// calendar first, panel UNDER it.
//
// THE PANEL IS UNDER THE CALENDAR ON A PHONE, not above it. Pinning it to the top
// of the viewport produces a 594px banner occupying 71% of an 844px screen - the
// banner reads as "too big to be sticking to the top". Pinning it is the mistake,
// not the size, and putting it under the calendar removes the problem structurally
// instead of shrinking a
// decoration until it is no longer worth showing. The banner is ALSO capped
// (see EventDetail.astro), because a cap is right on both sides of the
// breakpoint.
//
// Feedback for a tap does not depend on the panel being visible: the selected
// cell in the calendar shows that date's event NAME inline, immediately. The panel
// is the detail, not the confirmation.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE SELECTION UNIT IS A DAY
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// A calendar cell is a date, and two events can share a date. So the radio group
// is over DAYS, one input per distinct date, rendered once in a hidden group -
// and both the calendar cells and the list rows are `<label for>` pointing at
// them. Selecting from either view moves the same radio, so the two views can
// never disagree, and there is no JavaScript keeping them in sync.
//
// Selecting a date shows EVERY event on it, stacked in the panel, each with its
// own banner. No event is hidden and no banner is picked arbitrarily.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY THERE IS NO SCRIPT ON THIS PAGE
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Both mechanisms are CSS, exactly as the theme switcher already is:
//
//   view switch   html:has(#view-list:checked) .events__view--list { ... }
//   day selection html:has(#day-<key>:checked) :global([data-panel='<key>']) { ... }
//
// Which cannot flash, cannot desync from its own state, and works with scripting
// off. The cost is a per-day selector list, which is honest and test-enforced
// rather than hidden. See "THE PER-DAY SELECTORS" further down, including the
// point at which this design should be reconsidered.
//
// The one real dependency is `:has()`, Baseline from Chrome 105, Safari 15.4 and
// Firefox 121. That is OLDER than the `light-dark()` src/styles/themes.css already
// relies on, so it raises no new floor for this site.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

import PageShell from '../components/PageShell.astro'
import EventCalendar from '../components/EventCalendar.astro'
import EventDetail from '../components/EventDetail.astro'
import EventRow from '../components/EventRow.astro'
import {
  eventDays,
  dayKey,
  dayRadioLabel,
  isSelectableDay,
  monthGrids
} from '../lib/calendar'
import {
  eventCount,
  events,
  formatEventDate,
  formatEventDateSpan,
  groupByMonth,
  isPast,
  pageNote,
  reviewedOn,
  sortEvents
} from '../lib/events'

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Derive everything from the data. Nothing here is typed by hand.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

const ordered = sortEvents(events)

/**
 * One entry per date something happens on, ascending, each holding its events.
 *
 * This list ALSO holds dates that only exist because a multi-day event runs
 * THROUGH them - 20 to 31 October 2026, for the early voting window - and those
 * carry no events of their own. They are here so the month grid can mark them.
 * They are NOT selectable, and `selectableDays` below is what everything that
 * needs a control uses instead.
 */
const days = eventDays(ordered)

/**
 * The dates a reader can actually choose: the ones with at least one event on
 * them. A covered day is excluded.
 *
 * THIS IS THE LINE BETWEEN "the calendar tells the reader about this date" and
 * "the calendar offers the reader this date", and it is computed in one place
 * because four things depend on it - the radio group, the panels, the selected
 * date on first paint, and the generated per-day CSS below. Deriving it separately
 * in each is four chances to add a radio for a day with nothing behind it, and
 * that failure is worse than the one this page had before: the control would be
 * real, focusable, keyboard-operable and announced as a selectable date, and it
 * would open an empty panel.
 */
const selectableDays = days.filter((day) => isSelectableDay(day))

/**
 * How many dates exist only because an event runs through them.
 *
 * Drives the legend below. The legend is conditional on this being non-zero,
 * because a page that explains a marker nobody can see is a paragraph about
 * nothing - the same rule as the page note, which renders only when the data
 * carries one.
 */
const coveredOnlyDays = days.filter((day) => day.continues && !isSelectableDay(day))

/** Month grids, derived from those days - never a hardcoded month list. */
const months = monthGrids(days)

/**
 * Dates already past, taken from the data rather than from the clock.
 *
 * A day's first event decides it, which is unambiguous for a day with events on
 * it: every event on a date starts on that date, so they cannot disagree about
 * whether it has passed.
 *
 * A COVERED DAY HAS NO EVENT OF ITS OWN TO ASK, so it inherits the answer from the
 * event that starts the run - `covering[0]`, which `eventDays` sorted ascending, so
 * it is the earliest coverer. Without that, a fortnight of early voting that has
 * already happened would render as upcoming for every day after its start, which is
 * the mirror image of the bug that put covered days in the grid at all: there it
 * under-reported future access, and here it would over-report past access.
 *
 * BUILT IN A LOOP RATHER THAN IN A FILTER, and that is the load-bearing part. The
 * covered days need to look up a set that is still being built, so the two have to
 * be interleaved in date order - and `days` is ascending, so the owner is always
 * added before the days it covers. A `days.filter(...)` that read `pastKeys` inside
 * its own predicate would read the variable while its `const` initialiser was still
 * running, which throws a ReferenceError at MODULE SCOPE and takes the build down.
 */
const pastKeys = new Set<string>();
for (const day of days) {
  if (day.events.length > 0) {
    if (isPast(day.events[0], reviewedOn)) pastKeys.add(day.key);
    continue;
  }
  // A covered day: no event of its own, so it is past exactly when the run that
  // covers it is. `covering[0]` is the earliest coverer, because `eventDays` sorted
  // each list ascending by the covering event's own start.
  const owner = day.covering[0];
  if (owner === undefined) continue;
  if (pastKeys.has(dayKey(owner.startsAt))) pastKeys.add(day.key);
}

/**
 * The day selected on first paint: the first UPCOMING one, falling back to the
 * first of any kind. A calendar whose panel is empty on arrival is a page that
 * has not rendered.
 *
 * Note what is NOT consulted: the clock. The split between past and upcoming comes
 * from `reviewedOn`, a date a human puts in the file. Calling new Date() would make
 * the built HTML depend on the day the build ran, which is ROADMAP design rule 8
 * broken in its most literal way. See `reviewedOn` in src/lib/events-schema.ts.
 *
 * Drawn from `selectableDays`, NOT from `days`. A covered day has no panel to show,
 * so selecting it would leave the reader looking at an empty column beside a cell
 * they believe they chose.
 */
const selectedKey =
  (selectableDays.find((day) => !pastKeys.has(day.key)) ?? selectableDays[0])?.key ?? ''

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE PER-DAY SELECTORS, GENERATED
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// WHY THIS IS A STRING BUILT IN CODE. A test that enforces every date in the data
// appears in the CSS cannot go off until after the build is already broken, and it
// guards a coupling that does not need to exist.
//
// The contract it used to impose asked whoever edits the data to ALSO hand-edit CSS in an .astro
// file. That is a manual step with no compiler behind it, guarded only by a test
// that fires after the build already broke. A page whose interactivity is derived
// from its own data cannot go stale, so the coupling is removed rather than
// policed.
//
// WHY GENERATED CSS AT ALL, since the alternative is a script. A calendar cell and
// a list row are rendered by DIFFERENT components from the radio they select, and
// a panel is a sibling of both. CSS has no way to relate a label to a control it
// merely names, so the only way to say "this date is selected" is one selector per
// date. That is the price of zero JavaScript.
//
// Emitted into a separate `is:inline` block because that is the only Astro style
// block that takes a runtime string. The trade is real and worth naming: inline
// styles are NOT scoped, so every selector below must be written as it will
// appear in the document, with no page-scope class. That is also why `:global()`
// is absent here while the hand-written block above uses it - opposite mechanisms,
// opposite requirements. See the note on the hand-written block below.
//
// SCALING. This stays linear in the number of dates, which is fine at tens of
// events and honest to say is not fine at hundreds. At that point the right trade
// is one small inline script setting a data attribute on the selected cell, and
// the calendar should stop pretending otherwise.
//
// FROM `selectableDays`, NEVER FROM `days`. This is the fourth consumer of that
// split and the one that makes it matter most: a covered day emitted here would
// get `#day-2026-10-25`, a `data-day='2026-10-25'` target and a focus rule - for a
// date with no radio to check and no panel to show. The selectors would be dead,
// the cell would be marked, and nothing would say why. So the generator is fed the
// selectable days and can only ever describe dates that exist as controls.
const dayKeys = selectableDays.map((day) => day.key)

const selectorFor = (attr: string, pseudo: string) =>
  dayKeys.map((key) => `html:has(#day-${key}:${pseudo}) [data-${attr}='${key}']`).join(',\n  ')

const perDayCss = [
  `/* 1. The panel for the selected date becomes visible. */\n  ${selectorFor('panel', 'checked')} {\n    display: block;\n  }`,
  `/* 2. The selected date is marked on its calendar cell AND its list row. Marked by\n     a rule and a background, never by colour alone: a tint is the one signal that\n     disappears in forced-colours mode. */\n  ${selectorFor('day', 'checked')} {\n    background: var(--chip-bg);\n    border-inline-start: var(--rule-width-strong) solid var(--accent);\n  }`,
  `/* 3. The focus ring, on the same targets. The radio is clip-path: inset(50%), so\n     it cannot paint a ring on itself; the reader must SEE the label in the other\n     component highlighted. Absence of this rule cost the page its focus ring once. */\n  ${selectorFor('day', 'focus-visible')} {\n    outline: var(--focus-width) solid var(--focus);\n    outline-offset: calc(-1 * var(--focus-width));\n  }`,
].join('\n\n  ')

// The list view groups by month with the same helper the reading room uses, so both
// pages order dates identically.
const upcomingGroups = groupByMonth(ordered.filter((event) => !isPast(event, reviewedOn)))
const pastEvents = ordered.filter((event) => isPast(event, reviewedOn))

const firstEvent = ordered[0]
const lastEvent = ordered[ordered.length - 1]

// Spans, not single dates, on both ends. The last date is early voting's END, not
// its start: the reader asking "how late can I vote in person" is looking for the
// 31st, and a meta description that stops on the 3rd of November understates the
// window by more than three weeks.
const firstDate = firstEvent ? formatEventDateSpan(firstEvent.startsAt, firstEvent.endsAt) : ''
const lastDate = lastEvent
  ? formatEventDateSpan(lastEvent.startsAt, lastEvent.endsAt)
  : ''

// EVERY CLAIM IN HERE IS DERIVED FROM THE DATA, including this description. A
// sentence such as "Each row carries a thumbnail" is not safe to write by hand here:
// three of seven rows had none. A count is only trustworthy if it is computed.
//
// `selectableDays.length`, NOT `days.length`, and the difference is thirteen
// against six on the shipped data. `days` also holds the twelve dates inside the
// early voting window, and "across 18 dates" would tell a reader there are eighteen
// things to choose from when there are six - an over-count that runs in the
// optimistic direction, which is exactly as much of a lie as an under-count, and
// no easier to spot.
const description =
  `The events calendar: ${eventCount} dated events` +
  (firstDate && lastDate ? ` between ${firstDate} and ${lastDate}` : '') +
  ` across ${selectableDays.length} dates. Choose a date to see what is on.`

/** Render one row for an event, with the flags the list view needs. */
const rowFlags = (event: (typeof ordered)[number]) => {
  const key = dayKey(event.startsAt)
  const day = days.find((candidate) => candidate.key === key)
  return {
    dayKey: key,
    past: pastKeys.has(key),
    sharesDay: (day?.count ?? 0) > 1
  }
}
---

<PageShell title="Events" description={description}>
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1 class="page-head__title">Events</h1>

    {/*
      THIS HEADER BAR CARRIES THE REVIEW DATE AND NOTHING ELSE.

      NOTHING ELSE BELONGS HERE, and the two obvious candidates are excluded on
      purpose. A sentence explaining that the calendar and the list are two views of
      the same dates explains a switch to a reader: the switch is labelled "List
      view", it is a real switch, and it is one tab stop away. A provenance
      paragraph asking the reader to check our sources asserts a property that is
      visible instead: every event row links to the notice it came from, and a
      reader can check that by looking. A page that says "check our sources" while
      not obviously having them is worse than one that simply has them.

      The review date stays because it is the only thing on the page telling a
      reader when these dates were last checked against a source, and published
      election dates with no review date on them at all is a real loss rather than
      a tidying gain.
    */}
    {
      reviewedOn && (
        <p class="page-head__reviewed">
          Last reviewed{' '}
          <strong>
            <time datetime={reviewedOn}>{formatEventDate(reviewedOn)}</time>
          </strong>
        </p>
      )
    }

    {/*
      THE COVERED-DAY LEGEND, rendered only when there are covered days to explain.

      A marker with no legend is a marker a reader has to decode from the page's own
      visual grammar, and this page does not have one: there is nothing else on the
      calendar that looks like a rule beside a numeral. So the shape is named in
      words, once, near the calendar.

      CONDITIONAL, and the condition is `coveredOnlyDays.length > 0`. A page whose
      events are all single dates would otherwise open with a sentence about a mark
      that appears nowhere on it - which is the same defect as a description
      claiming every row has a thumbnail, and the test on that description exists
      because it shipped.

      IT LIVES HERE AND NOT IN EventCalendar.astro, and that is forced rather than
      chosen: the marker is drawn by the component, but this is PROSE about the
      component's output, and a rule in a page's scoped style block cannot style
      another component's elements anyway - it fails SILENTLY. See the note at the
      foot of the calendar's own style block.

      NO TAG NAME IS SPELLED IN THIS COMMENT, and that is not fussiness. The suite
      that reads this page's style block finds it with a non-greedy tag pattern, so
      a literal style tag inside an earlier comment captures the block from the
      wrong place and mis-pairs its comment delimiters - which makes the page appear
      to name a class it does not style, and turns a 200-character aside into a red
      build. Prose about a tag refers to the block, not to its punctuation.

      THE BRACE AFTER THIS COMMENT IS LOAD-BEARING, and it is worth naming because it
      looks like a typo. An Astro JSX comment is an EXPRESSION container holding a
      comment, not a self-closing comment: drop the closing brace and the container
      simply continues into whatever expression follows, so the block after it stops
      being a sibling and the build dies with "Expected } but found ." - an error
      that points 30 lines away from the cause.

      THE DELIMITERS ARE DESCRIBED IN WORDS, NEVER WRITTEN OUT, and the reason is
      concrete: spelling them literally CLOSES THE COMMENT AT THAT POINT, and
      everything from there to the real terminator is rendered as visible page text -
      about 500 words of a note about braces, printed where a reader would see it.
      That survives because every test asserts on structure and nobody reads the page's
      prose for content that was supposed to be absent. The rule: never spell a comment
      delimiter inside a comment. Say "the opening brace and slash-star" and move on.
      test/no-comment-leak.test.mjs fails the build if a delimiter appears inside
      comment prose anywhere in src/.

      One sentence, not a paragraph of instructions: the mark is right there on the
      page, so explaining how to use it in prose would be teaching the reader
      something they can see. The test asserts this element exists and says what the
      mark means, so the assertion is about the mark being explained, not about how
      many sentences it takes.
    */}
    {
      coveredOnlyDays.length > 0 && (
        <p class="page-head__legend">
          A short rule beside a date marks a day inside a multi-day event. The event
          is listed on the day it starts, with its full range.
        </p>
      )
    }

    {/*
      NO PROVENANCE PARAGRAPH, and the rule behind that is worth keeping.

      "Every listing links to the notice it was taken from, so you can check it
      rather than take our word for it" is sound reasoning - a page of real election
      dates should say where they came from - but every event carries a `url`, so
      the paragraph describes a property the page DEMONSTRATES. Every row has an
      outbound link to scvotes.gov, to the organiser, or to the party notice. A
      reader can see the sourcing rather than take the site's word that sourcing
      exists, and prose asserting "check our sources" is worth less than sources a
      reader can actually click.

      What that costs, and it is a real cost: the explicit warning that a schedule
      may have moved since the date above. That remains true and is not stated in
      the header. It is recorded in data/events.schema.md, which is where a rule
      about the data belongs.

      There is deliberately still no claim here about who curates or reviewed the
      list. The footer credits the curator, so that attribution exists once, in one
      place, without being asserted twice.
    */}
  </div>

  {/*
    THE VIEW SWITCH, and why a checkbox rather than two radios.

    The brief asked for a Boolean switch, and a checkbox with role="switch" is
    that: one control, one tab stop, Space toggles it, and the state is announced
    as on/off rather than as "one of two buttons". Two radios would model the
    choice more precisely and cost one extra tab stop; the single control wins
    because there is nothing to choose BETWEEN at the moment of toggling.

    It is a real <input type="checkbox">, visually replaced by the track and thumb
    below, and it stays focusable and in the accessibility tree.
  */}
  <fieldset class="switch">
    <legend class="label">View</legend>
    <label class="switch__option" for="view-list">
      <input type="checkbox" role="switch" id="view-list" class="switch__input" />
      <span class="switch__track" aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="switch__thumb"></span>
      </span>
      <span class="switch__text">List view</span>
    </label>
  </fieldset>

  {/*
    THE DAY RADIOS. One per distinct date, rendered ONCE, here.

    They are the single source of the selection, which is what lets the calendar
    and the list share it without any script. They are visually hidden rather than
    `display: none`, because a hidden input is removed from keyboard navigation
    and from the accessibility tree - and this control is the only way to reach
    every event on the page.

    `aria-label` is set explicitly rather than relying on an associated <label>.
    A radio here is pointed at by MANY labels - one calendar cell plus one list row
    per event on that date - and with several labels a browser concatenates them
    all into an unreadable accessible name. Naming it directly makes the name the
    same in both views, which is correct, because both views select DATES.

    RENDERED FROM `selectableDays`, not from `days`, and this is the single most
    consequential line in the file for the shape of the controls. `days` also holds
    the dates inside the early voting window; a radio for each of those would be
    twelve focusable, announced, checkable controls that open a panel containing
    nothing - a reader arrowing through the calendar would be told "25 October 2026,
    0 events" and shown a blank column. That is worse than the original defect,
    because it presents broken machinery as working machinery.

    `dayRadioLabel` therefore only ever sees a day with at least one event, which is
    why its count is never zero and its plural is always honest.
  */}
  <div class="events__inputs">
    {
      selectableDays.map((day) => (
        <input
          class="events__day"
          type="radio"
          name="sb-day"
          id={`day-${day.key}`}
          value={day.key}
          checked={day.key === selectedKey}
          aria-controls={`panel-${day.key}`}
          aria-label={dayRadioLabel(day)}
        />
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <div class="events__layout">
    {/*
      THE NAVIGATION COLUMN: calendar and list are two views of one thing, and
      exactly one is displayed. Both are rendered, so flipping the switch is a
      repaint and not a fetch or a re-render.
    */}
    <section class="events__nav" id="events-calendar" aria-label="Choose a date">
      <div class="events__view events__view--calendar">
        <EventCalendar months={months} pastKeys={[...pastKeys]} />
      </div>

      <div class="events__view events__view--list">
        {
          pastEvents.length > 0 && (
            <section class="events-section events-section--past" aria-labelledby="events-past">
              <h2 class="events-section__title" id="events-past">
                Already held
              </h2>
              <ul class="events__list">
                {pastEvents.map((event) => (
                  <EventRow event={event} {...rowFlags(event)} />
                ))}
              </ul>
            </section>
          )
        }

        {
          upcomingGroups.length > 0 ? (
            <section class="events-section" aria-labelledby="events-upcoming">
              <h2 class="events-section__title" id="events-upcoming">
                Upcoming
              </h2>
              {upcomingGroups.map((group) => (
                <div class="events-month">
                  <h3 class="events-month__title">{group.label}</h3>
                  <ul class="events__list">
                    {group.events.map((event) => (
                      <EventRow event={event} {...rowFlags(event)} />
                    ))}
                  </ul>
                </div>
              ))}
            </section>
          ) : (
            pastEvents.length > 0 && (
              <p class="events-none">
                Nothing upcoming is listed at the moment. Dates appear here as they are
                confirmed.
              </p>
            )
          )
        }
      </div>
    </section>

    {/*
      THE PANEL. One region, one announced name, one block per DAY inside it -
      only one of which is visible. A day with several events renders several
      EventDetail blocks, stacked.

      aria-live="polite" is what makes it usable rather than merely correct:
      selecting a date moves the :checked state, and CSS alone cannot tell a screen
      reader that the region now describes a different day. Deliberately NOT
      aria-atomic: with atomic set, every selection would re-announce the whole
      region including the banner alt text, and the day name - which is what
      actually changed.

      ONE PANEL PER SELECTABLE DAY, and that is why the list above is `days` and
      this one is `selectableDays`. A panel exists to be shown by exactly one
      `:checked` selector, and the generated selector list contains no covered day.
      An extra hidden panel would be dead markup for every date inside a span, and
      its contents would still be in the accessibility tree under a region that is
      announced as live - so a screen reader could encounter a fortnight of voting
      text that the page can never show on purpose.
    */}
    <aside class="events__panel" aria-label="Selected date" aria-live="polite">
      {
        selectableDays.map((day) => (
          <div class="panel" id={`panel-${day.key}`} data-panel={day.key} aria-label={day.label}>
            {pastKeys.has(day.key) && <p class="panel__flag">Already held</p>}
            {day.events.map((event, index) => (
              <EventDetail
                event={event}
                headingLevel={index === 0 ? 'h2' : 'h3'}
                showBackLink={index === day.events.length - 1}
                backTargetId="events-calendar"
              />
            ))}
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </aside>
  </div>

  {/* ------------------------------------------------------------------------
      THE CLOSING NOTE.

      Rendered from `pageNote` in data/events.json, at the very bottom, after the
      calendar and before the usage note. It is the client's prose about ongoing
      campaign activity, which is deliberately NOT a dated event: putting it on the
      calendar would tell a reader it happens on a date, and it does not.

      The heading is a real `<h2>` and the section is labelled by it, so the block
      appears in the document outline as what it is - a section of the page - rather
      than as trailing text belonging to whatever precedes it.

      RENDERED ONLY WHEN THE BLOCK IS ABSENT-FREE. `pageNote` is null when the file
      has no block, and the schema rejects an empty paragraphs array, so there is no
      path here that produces a heading with nothing under it.
  */}
  {
    pageNote && (
      <section class="closing" aria-labelledby="closing-heading">
        <h2 class="closing__heading" id="closing-heading">
          {pageNote.heading}
        </h2>
        <div class="closing__body">
          {pageNote.paragraphs.map((text) => (
            <p class="closing__text">{text}</p>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>
    )
  }

  <p class="events-note">
    Choosing a date swaps the panel without reloading the page, and works with
    scripting switched off. Use the arrow keys to move between dates.
  </p>
</PageShell>

{/*
  The per-day selectors, generated from `days`. This block is `is:inline` because
  that is the only Astro style block that accepts a runtime string, and inline
  means UNSCOPED: the selectors below are written exactly as they appear in the
  document. See the long note in the scoped <style> below for why the three rules
  exist at all, and why `:global()` is absent here but present there.
*/}
<style is:inline set:html={perDayCss} />

<style>
  .page-head {
    padding-block-end: var(--space-5);
    margin-block-end: var(--space-4);
    border-block-end: var(--rule-width) solid var(--rule);
  }

  .page-head__title {
    font-size: var(--size-3xl);
  }

  /* The review date, the ONLY text in this header bar.

     It is a top-level `p`, so the display and the top margin both go:
     a block-level paragraph already sits on its own line, and `display: block` on a
     `p` is a no-op. Muted and small, because it is metadata about the page rather
     than content ON the page. */
  .page-head__reviewed {
    margin-block: 0;
    font-size: var(--size-sm);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  /* The covered-day legend. Rendered from `coveredOnlyDays`, so it appears only when
     there is at least one covered day, and never on a calendar where every event is
     a single date.

     It is styled HERE rather than in EventCalendar.astro for the reason the calendar
     spells out at the foot of its own style block: a rule in this page's scoped block
     carries this page's scope id, and the elements it would need to match are
     rendered by another component under a different one. Written here, this rule
     would match the legend paragraph - which this page renders - and nothing else,
